1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,997
Asking Contentious Questions.

2
00:00:01,997 --> 00:00:06,794
Imagine you're having coffee with someone
you've recently become friends with.

3
00:00:06,794 --> 00:00:11,529
You're getting to know each other, and
mid-conversation you ask: "Hey, so I'm

4
00:00:11,529 --> 00:00:14,582
curious about something — what's your net
worth?".

5
00:00:14,582 --> 00:00:19,641
Everyone knows you don't ask that. Not
because the answer doesn't matter — but

6
00:00:19,641 --> 00:00:24,898
because there's no context for why you're
asking, and no trust around what you'll

7
00:00:24,898 --> 00:00:26,146
do with the answer.

8
00:00:26,146 --> 00:00:32,148
Now bring that instinct into a discovery
call. "Are you profitable?" is the most

9
00:00:32,148 --> 00:00:38,074
important question you can ask — but also
the one prospects are least likely to

10
00:00:38,074 --> 00:00:41,797
answer honestly, unless you give them a
reason to.

11
00:00:41,797 --> 00:00:46,837
That's why it's helpful to provide
context when you ask a question. One

12
00:00:46,837 --> 00:00:51,085
phrase makes the difference: "the reason
I ask is because.".

13
00:00:51,085 --> 00:00:56,101
Here's why it works. It's easy — no
special skill or finesse required to

14
00:00:56,101 --> 00:01:01,329
execute it. It's empathetic — you're
demonstrating self-awareness by making

15
00:01:01,329 --> 00:01:06,981
sure your question and the reason behind
it are clear and well-received. The word

16
00:01:06,981 --> 00:01:12,562
"because" creates certainty in the mind
of your prospect as to why you're asking

17
00:01:12,562 --> 00:01:17,932
and what you'll do with the answer. That
comfort makes it more likely they'll

18
00:01:17,932 --> 00:01:19,274
respond truthfully.

19
00:01:19,274 --> 00:01:23,894
When you append that phrase to a
contentious question, you remove the

20
00:01:23,894 --> 00:01:29,262
ambiguity. The prospect knows why you're
asking and how the answer will be used.

21
00:01:29,262 --> 00:01:32,184
There are three situations where it
applies.

22
00:01:32,184 --> 00:01:36,039
**Situation one: while asking the
question.**.

23
00:01:36,039 --> 00:01:40,879
Here's what it would look like: "Are you
profitable, or on your way to

24
00:01:40,879 --> 00:01:45,650
profitability? The reason I ask is
because companies that are already

25
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:50,981
profitable can typically access our
extended payment terms. But if you're not

26
00:01:50,981 --> 00:01:56,593
there yet, our Net-1 charge card is still
the strongest option on the market — up

27
00:01:56,593 --> 00:01:59,399
to 3% cashback, no personal guarantee.".

28
00:01:59,399 --> 00:02:05,906
Watch how Cameron Baker does this without
using the words "the reason I ask." On a

29
00:02:05,906 --> 00:02:11,931
call with Walli Cases — a deal that
closed at $400K — because he starts with

30
00:02:11,931 --> 00:02:13,377
the reason itself:

31
00:02:13,377 --> 00:02:20,550
"It's more
optimized for businesses that are in

32
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:27,433
their profitability, their cash
conversion cycles in check. Where do you

33
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:33,346
guys fall with profitability? Are you
guys profitable today?".

34
00:02:33,346 --> 00:02:39,009
Cameron frames the question as fit —
"where do you fall" — not qualification.

35
00:02:39,009 --> 00:02:42,959
The prospect doesn't feel tested. They
feel consulted.

36
00:02:42,959 --> 00:02:46,953
**Situation two: when you didn't like the
answer you got.**.

37
00:02:46,953 --> 00:02:52,436
Prospects dodge. They say "it depends" or
"we're scaling" or "it's complicated."

38
00:02:52,436 --> 00:02:57,642
That's when you deploy the reasoning
phrase again — but this time, lower the

39
00:02:57,642 --> 00:02:59,585
commitment of your question.

40
00:02:59,585 --> 00:03:05,080
"Totally understand. The reason I ask is
because you mentioned wanting to spend

41
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:10,082
$30K a day on ads — the cash position
helps me figure out whether we can

42
00:03:10,082 --> 00:03:15,648
support that volume on a float card or
whether Net-1 is the better fit for right

43
00:03:15,648 --> 00:03:16,071
now.".

44
00:03:16,071 --> 00:03:21,232
You're anchoring to their own goal. They
told you what they want. You're

45
00:03:21,232 --> 00:03:24,430
explaining why the question serves that
goal.

46
00:03:24,430 --> 00:03:31,218
Ciaran Lynch shows what lowering the
commitment looks like:

47
00:03:31,218 --> 00:03:39,154
"Was that like a one
month profitability or are you guys

48
00:03:39,154 --> 00:03:43,749
consistently profitable month on month
now?".

49
00:03:43,749 --> 00:03:49,318
He's not asking for the full financial
history. He's narrowing the question to

50
00:03:49,318 --> 00:03:54,815
something easier to answer — were you
profitable recently? That's a great way

51
00:03:54,815 --> 00:04:00,167
to state your intention, which is
understanding whether Net-1 is a fit. And

52
00:04:00,167 --> 00:04:05,808
for Net-1, that's really all you need to
know: that they have cash in the bank.

53
00:04:05,808 --> 00:04:09,012
**Situation three: responding to an
objection.**.

54
00:04:09,012 --> 00:04:13,927
Sometimes a prospect doesn't dodge — they
refuse. "I'm not comfortable sharing

55
00:04:13,927 --> 00:04:18,459
that." This is where you combine the
first two situations: give them the

56
00:04:18,459 --> 00:04:23,501
reason you're asking, and ask a version
of the question with a lower commitment.

57
00:04:23,501 --> 00:04:29,044
"Totally fair. The reason we ask about
profitability is because it helps us

58
00:04:29,044 --> 00:04:34,736
calibrate which product fits you best.
Were you profitable last month? That's

59
00:04:34,736 --> 00:04:37,433
really all we need to get started.".

60
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:42,666
Instead of asking for twelve months of
profitability data, you're asking about

61
00:04:42,666 --> 00:04:47,831
one month. You've explained why the
question matters, and you've made it easy

62
00:04:47,831 --> 00:04:48,579
to say yes.

63
00:04:48,579 --> 00:04:51,690
**Additional questions to strengthen your
discovery.**.

64
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:59,318
When asking about cash position, James
Heflin goes direct:

65
00:04:59,318 --> 00:05:08,496
"What we're looking
at is, do you have the cash on hand?"

66
00:05:08,496 --> 00:05:17,078
Nick Erdman frames it as a product
choice: "A

67
00:05:17,078 --> 00:05:25,898
lot of customers say, hey, we have the
cash on hand and they can do the net

68
00:05:25,898 --> 00:05:26,613
one.".

69
00:05:26,613 --> 00:05:33,443
When asking about existing debt, Kurt
Bell uses the option tactic — giving the

70
00:05:33,443 --> 00:05:40,007
prospect two closed-ended choices instead
of an open-ended question:

71
00:05:40,007 --> 00:05:47,103
"Are you paying that
in cash or are you leveraging a line of

72
00:05:47,103 --> 00:05:54,199
credit to pay down those invoices?" Then
he probes further: "If you don't mind me

73
00:05:54,199 --> 00:06:00,142
asking, what's the interest rate on that
line of credit right now?".

74
00:06:00,142 --> 00:06:05,878
Here's what we covered. Three situations
for deploying "the reason I ask is

75
00:06:05,878 --> 00:06:11,770
because" — while asking the question,
when you get a vague answer, and when a

76
00:06:11,770 --> 00:06:17,118
prospect pushes back. In each case,
you're doing two things: providing

77
00:06:17,118 --> 00:06:22,855
context for why you're asking, and
lowering the commitment of your question

78
00:06:22,855 --> 00:06:29,056
when needed. Cameron Baker leads with the
reason without using the phrase. Ciaran

79
00:06:29,056 --> 00:06:34,637
Lynch narrows a vague answer into
something actionable. James Heflin goes

80
00:06:34,637 --> 00:06:39,521
direct on cash. Kurt Bell uses
closed-ended options to make debt

81
00:06:39,521 --> 00:06:41,149
conversations easier.

82
00:06:41,149 --> 00:06:46,640
Getting answers to financing question can
be challenging but with some framing it

83
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:51,994
can be quickest ways to flag a deal that
won't make it through underwriting. If

84
00:06:51,994 --> 00:06:56,799
you take one new tactic from this
training, make it "the reason I ask".
